WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.140
Why should stretching a membrane open a channel at all?

2
00:00:07.083 --> 00:00:09.383
Start with the simplest picture that could work.

3
00:00:10.183 --> 00:00:12.063
The channel is either closed or open.

4
00:00:13.017 --> 00:00:16.337
The open state takes up more room in the plane of the membrane.

5
00:00:17.277 --> 00:00:19.037
Call the extra amount the area gained.

6
00:00:20.050 --> 00:00:22.470
Membrane tension is an in-plane stretch.

7
00:00:23.350 --> 00:00:25.130
It is force per unit length of membrane.

8
00:00:26.250 --> 00:00:29.930
Stretching a membrane does work on anything in it that gets wider.

9
00:00:30.770 --> 00:00:33.090
That work is tension times the area gained.

10
00:00:34.133 --> 00:00:38.853
So tension lowers the energy of the open state, relative to the closed one.

11
00:00:39.817 --> 00:00:45.317
Write it out. The energy difference equals the resting preference for closed, minus

12
00:00:45.317 --> 00:00:47.277
tension times the area gained.

13
00:00:48.350 --> 00:00:51.510
In a slack membrane, the channel prefers to be closed.

14
00:00:52.390 --> 00:00:54.510
As tension rises, that preference shrinks.

15
00:00:55.483 --> 00:00:58.623
At one particular tension, the preference disappears.

16
00:00:59.763 --> 00:01:01.043
Above it, the open state wins.

17
00:01:02.117 --> 00:01:05.477
Two channels are each open half the time at the same tension.

18
00:01:06.357 --> 00:01:08.857
One gains five square nanometres on opening.

19
00:01:09.737 --> 00:01:10.917
The other gains three hundred.

20
00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:16.980
Which one goes from mostly closed to mostly open over the narrower range of tension?

21
00:01:23.133 --> 00:01:24.573
The three hundred channel.

22
00:01:25.373 --> 00:01:29.473
Each small rise in tension gives it sixty times the energy advantage.

23
00:01:30.567 --> 00:01:34.747
So a small change in tension swings it all the way from closed to open.

24
00:01:38.750 --> 00:01:43.350
To turn the energy into a curve, use the Boltzmann relation from statistical

25
00:01:43.350 --> 00:01:44.350
physics.

26
00:01:44.900 --> 00:01:49.580
For a two-state channel, the ratio of open to closed depends on the energy

27
00:01:49.580 --> 00:01:51.840
difference, compared with thermal energy.

28
00:01:52.883 --> 00:01:57.423
For a two-state channel at equilibrium, this gives the fraction of channels open.

29
00:01:58.483 --> 00:02:00.243
Patch papers fit the same curve to current.

30
00:02:01.063 --> 00:02:03.743
That reading assumes current tracks the fraction open.

31
00:02:04.503 --> 00:02:09.123
The curve has two fitted numbers: a half-activation tension and a slope factor.

32
00:02:10.167 --> 00:02:13.007
Match the two forms, and each number gets a meaning.

33
00:02:13.867 --> 00:02:18.647
The half-activation tension is the resting preference for closed, divided by the

34
00:02:18.647 --> 00:02:19.647
area gained.

35
00:02:20.300 --> 00:02:24.240
The slope factor is thermal energy, divided by the area gained.

36
00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:28.280
More area means a smaller slope factor, and a steeper switch.

37
00:02:29.283 --> 00:02:35.103
One unit note. Piconewtons per nanometre and millinewtons per metre are the same

38
00:02:35.103 --> 00:02:36.103
quantity.

39
00:02:36.617 --> 00:02:37.997
That is the one idea.

40
00:02:39.017 --> 00:02:40.037
Tension pays for area.

41
00:02:40.897 --> 00:02:43.557
In a two-state model, the area sets the steepness.
